.TH ATLAS-FIND 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-find \- Walk a folder tree and list (or act on) files that match conditions such as name, size, type, time or permissions.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
find  site  \-type f  \-name '*.js'  \-size \-1k  \-exec wc \-c {} \e;
 |     |      |         |            |           '\-\-\- run a command; {} is the file; \e; ends it
 |     |      |         |            '\-\-\- tests are ANDed together in order
 |     |      |         '\-\-\- match the name against a glob (quote it)
 |     |      '\-\-\- f = regular file, d = directory, l = symlink
 |     '\-\-\- where to start looking
 '\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Locate a file when you only know part of its name, its type or when it changed.
.IP \(bu 2
Privilege escalation checks: world\-writable files, SUID binaries, files owned by a user.
.IP \(bu 2
Run a command on every matching file (\fB\-exec\fR).
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-name / \-iname\fR
Match the file name with a glob (\fB\-iname\fR ignores case).
.br
Use it when: You remember part of the name.
.TP
\fB\-type f|d|l\fR
Regular file, directory or symlink.
.br
Use it when: Skip folders or find links.
.TP
\fB\-maxdepth N / \-mindepth N\fR
Limit how deep to look.
.br
Use it when: Avoid huge trees.
.TP
\fB\-size +1M / \-1k\fR
Bigger / smaller than a size (\fBc\fR bytes, \fBk\fR, \fBM\fR).
.br
Use it when: Find big dumps or tiny stubs.
.TP
\fB\-mtime N / \-mmin N\fR
Modified N days / minutes ago (\fB\-1\fR = less than, \fB+1\fR = more than).
.br
Use it when: What changed during the incident?
.TP
\fB\-newer FILE\fR
Newer than another file.
.br
Use it when: What changed after the first alert?
.TP
\fB\-perm \-4000 / \-perm /111\fR
Has all of these bits / any of these bits.
.br
Use it when: SUID files, executable files.
.TP
\fB\-user NAME / \-group NAME\fR
Owned by.
.br
Use it when: Files an attacker or service user created.
.TP
\fB\-empty\fR
Empty files and folders.
.br
Use it when: Clean\-up or odd leftovers.
.TP
\fB\-exec CMD {} \e; / \-exec CMD {} +\fR
Run CMD per file / on batches of files.
.br
Use it when: Search or hash every match.
.TP
\fB\-print0 | xargs \-0\fR
Null\-separated output for safe piping.
.br
Use it when: File names with spaces.
.TP
\fB\-delete\fR
Delete matches (dangerous: test first with \fB\-print\fR).
.br
Use it when: Clean\-up only.
.TP
\fB! / \-not / \-o\fR
NOT / OR combinators (group with \fB\e( \e)\fR).
.br
Use it when: More complex conditions.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. List everything below a folder"
With no tests, find prints every path. \fBsort\fR makes the order stable (find prints in disk order).
.PP
.nf
$ find site | sort
.fi
.PP
.nf
site
site/.env
site/css
site/css/main.css
site/index.html
site/js
site/js/app.js
site/robots.txt
site/uploads
site/uploads/report.pdf
.fi
.PP
Folders and files, including the hidden \fB.env\fR.
.SS "2. By name, any depth"
\fB\-name\fR takes a glob. Quote it so the shell does not expand it first.
.PP
.nf
$ find . \-name '*.js' | sort
.fi
.PP
.nf
\&./site/js/app.js
.fi
.PP
One JavaScript file. \fB\-iname\fR would also catch \fBAPP.JS\fR.
.SS "3. Hidden files only"
Names starting with a dot are hidden from plain \fBls\fR. Config files and secrets often live there.
.PP
.nf
$ find site \-name '.*' \-type f
.fi
.PP
.nf
site/.env
.fi
.PP
The \fB.env\fR file appears.
.SS "4. Executable files"
\fB/111\fR means any execute bit is set. \fB\-maxdepth 1\fR stays in this folder.
.PP
.nf
$ find . \-maxdepth 1 \-type f \-perm /111 | sort
.fi
.PP
.nf
\&./hello
\&./run.sh
.fi
.PP
The compiled \fBhello\fR and the script \fBrun.sh\fR. In a real privilege\-escalation check you search \fB\-perm \-4000\fR for SUID.
.SS "5. By age"
\fB\-newer\fR compares modification times with a reference file. I set two files to an older date on purpose.
.PP
.nf
$ find site \-type f \-newer site/robots.txt | sort
.fi
.PP
.nf
site/.env
site/css/main.css
site/js/app.js
site/uploads/report.pdf
.fi
.PP
Files modified after \fBrobots.txt\fR: everything except the two aged files. Use this to find what changed after an alert.
.SS "6. Run a command on each match"
\fB\-exec\fR replaces \fB{}\fR with each file name. \fB\e;\fR runs the command once per file; \fB+\fR batches many files into one run.
.PP
.nf
$ find site \-type f \-exec grep \-l 'x' {} \e; | sort
.fi
.PP
.nf
site/uploads/report.pdf
.fi
.PP
The names of files that contain the letter x.
.SS "7. Safe piping with spaces in names"
Without \fB\-print0\fR/\fB\-0\fR, a name with a space splits into two arguments and breaks the next command.
.PP
.nf
$ touch 'a file.txt'; find . \-name '*.txt' \-print0 | xargs \-0 wc \-c | tail \-3
.fi
.PP
.nf
  0 ./a file.txt
103 ./holiday.txt
471 total
.fi
.PP
\fBwc\fR reports on \fBa file.txt\fR correctly because the names were separated by NUL bytes.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Quote globs: \fBfind . \-name *.js\fR can be expanded by the shell and fail.
.IP \(bu 2
Order matters for performance, not logic: put cheap tests (\fB\-type\fR, \fB\-name\fR) before \fB\-exec\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-mtime \-1\fR means less than 24 hours ago, \fB\-mtime 1\fR means exactly 1 day: easy to mix up.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-delete\fR placed before the tests deletes everything: always run with \fB\-print\fR first.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfind /\fR as a normal user prints many \fBPermission denied\fR lines; add \fB2>/dev/null\fR.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Find every file in \fBsite\fR larger than 5 bytes.
.IP 2. 3
List only directories two levels deep.
.IP 3. 3
Find files owned by your user that are not readable by others.
.SH SEE ALSO
locate, fd, ls \-la, xargs, stat, du.
.SH CAPTURE
Output captured 2026\-11\-23 with: find (GNU findutils) 4.9.0
